# RANDOM FOREST REGRESSION

==================================================
1. DEFINITION
==================================================

Random Forest Regression is a supervised learning algorithm
used to predict a CONTINUOUS target variable.

It is an ENSEMBLE algorithm that combines the predictions
of MANY Decision Trees to produce one final prediction.

Basic idea:

Many Decision Trees
        ↓
Combine their predictions
        ↓
Final Prediction


Example:

Tree 1 → ₹48,000
Tree 2 → ₹52,000
Tree 3 → ₹50,000
Tree 4 → ₹51,000
Tree 5 → ₹49,000

Final prediction:

(48 + 52 + 50 + 51 + 49) / 5
= ₹50,000


==================================================
2. WHY RANDOM FOREST?
==================================================

A single Decision Tree can easily overfit the training data.

Decision Tree:

Training Data
     ↓
One Tree
     ↓
Can become very complex
     ↓
Possible Overfitting


Random Forest:

Training Data
     ↓
Many different Decision Trees
     ↓
Combine their predictions
     ↓
More stable prediction


The main idea is:

Instead of depending on ONE tree,
use MANY trees and combine them.


==================================================
3. WHAT IS AN ENSEMBLE?
==================================================

Ensemble Learning means combining multiple models
to produce a stronger overall model.

Example:

Model 1 ──┐
Model 2 ──┤
Model 3 ──┼──→ Combined Prediction
Model 4 ──┤
Model 5 ──┘

Random Forest is an ensemble of Decision Trees.


==================================================
4. BASIC INTUITION
==================================================

Imagine asking 100 different experts to estimate
the price of a house.

Expert 1 → ₹50L
Expert 2 → ₹53L
Expert 3 → ₹48L
Expert 4 → ₹51L
...
Expert 100 → ₹49L

Instead of trusting one expert, take the average.

Random Forest works with the same basic idea:

Many Decision Trees
        ↓
Each gives a prediction
        ↓
Average the predictions
        ↓
Final prediction


==================================================
5. HOW RANDOM FOREST CREATES MANY TREES
==================================================

Random Forest introduces RANDOMNESS in two major ways:

1. Random samples of training data
2. Random subsets of features


Training Dataset
       ↓
Random Sampling
       ↓
 ┌─────┼─────┬─────┐
 ↓     ↓     ↓     ↓
Tree1 Tree2 Tree3 Tree4 ...
 └─────┼─────┴─────┘
       ↓
Combine predictions
       ↓
Final Prediction


==================================================
6. BOOTSTRAP SAMPLING
==================================================

Bootstrap sampling means creating different training datasets
by randomly sampling observations from the original dataset
WITH REPLACEMENT.

Example:

Original:

A B C D E

Possible bootstrap samples:

Tree 1:
A C C D E

Tree 2:
B B C D A

Tree 3:
A D E E B

Each tree receives a different sample.

Because sampling is WITH replacement,
the same observation can appear multiple times.


==================================================
7. RANDOM FEATURE SELECTION
==================================================

Random Forest also randomly considers a subset of features
when looking for a split.

Suppose we have:

10 features

At a particular split, the tree may consider only:

3 randomly selected features.

Example:

All features:

Age
Salary
Area
Bedrooms
Experience
Location
Education
Distance
Size
Rooms

At one split:

Randomly selected:

Age
Area
Experience

The tree chooses the best split among those features.


This introduces more diversity between trees.


==================================================
8. WHY RANDOMNESS HELPS
==================================================

If every tree were exactly the same, combining them would
provide little additional benefit.

Randomness makes trees different.

Different trees
      ↓
Different errors
      ↓
Combine predictions
      ↓
Errors can partially cancel out
      ↓
More stable model


This is one of the main reasons Random Forest works well.


==================================================
9. HOW A RANDOM FOREST TREE IS BUILT
==================================================

For each tree:

1. Take a bootstrap sample of the training data.

2. Start building a Decision Tree.

3. At each split, randomly select a subset of features.

4. Find the best split among those selected features.

5. Continue growing the tree according to the stopping
   conditions.

6. Repeat for many trees.


Flow:

Training Data
     ↓
Bootstrap Sample
     ↓
Random Feature Selection
     ↓
Build Decision Tree
     ↓
Repeat many times
     ↓
Many Trees


==================================================
10. FINAL PREDICTION
==================================================

For REGRESSION:

The final prediction is commonly the AVERAGE of the
predictions from all trees.

Example:

Tree 1 → 100
Tree 2 → 110
Tree 3 → 90
Tree 4 → 105
Tree 5 → 95

Final:

(100 + 110 + 90 + 105 + 95) / 5

= 100


Therefore:

Final Prediction
=
Average of Tree Predictions


==================================================
11. RANDOM FOREST REGRESSION vs
    DECISION TREE REGRESSION
==================================================

Decision Tree:

Dataset
   ↓
ONE Decision Tree
   ↓
Prediction


Random Forest:

Dataset
   ↓
Many Decision Trees
   ↓
Tree 1 → Prediction
Tree 2 → Prediction
Tree 3 → Prediction
...
Tree N → Prediction
   ↓
Average
   ↓
Final Prediction


Core difference:

Decision Tree
→ One tree

Random Forest
→ Many trees + randomness + aggregation


==================================================
12. OVERFITTING
==================================================

A single Decision Tree can overfit because it can become
too closely fitted to the training data.

Random Forest reduces this problem by combining many
different trees.

Conceptually:

One complex tree
→ High variance

Many diverse trees
→ Averaging reduces variance


Important:

Random Forest can still overfit if its configuration is
poor, but it is generally more resistant to overfitting
than a single unrestricted Decision Tree.


==================================================
13. BIAS AND VARIANCE
==================================================

Decision Tree:

Can have:
→ Low bias
→ High variance


Random Forest:

Averages many trees.

Averaging generally:
→ Reduces variance
→ Produces more stable predictions


This is a major reason Random Forest is powerful.


==================================================
14. FEATURE SCALING
==================================================

Random Forest generally does NOT require:

- Standardization
- Normalization

Why?

Decision Trees use threshold-based splits.

Example:

Age < 30

or:

Salary < 50000

Therefore, the model is generally not sensitive to feature
scale in the way that distance-based or gradient-based
models can be.


==================================================
15. SCIKIT-LEARN IMPLEMENTATION
==================================================

Import:

from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor


Create model:

model = RandomForestRegressor(
    n_estimators=100,
    random_state=42
)


Train:

model.fit(X_train, y_train)


Predict:

y_pred = model.predict(X_test)


Complete example:

from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor

model = RandomForestRegressor(
    n_estimators=100,
    random_state=42
)

model.fit(X_train, y_train)

y_pred = model.predict(X_test)


==================================================
16. IMPORTANT HYPERPARAMETERS
==================================================

1. n_estimators

Number of Decision Trees in the forest.

Example:

n_estimators = 100

→ 100 trees.

More trees can improve stability, but they also increase
training and prediction cost.


2. max_depth

Maximum depth of each Decision Tree.

Small value:
→ simpler trees

Large value:
→ more complex trees


3. min_samples_split

Minimum number of samples required to split an
internal node.


4. min_samples_leaf

Minimum number of samples required in a leaf.


5. max_features

Number or proportion of features considered when
searching for a split.


6. random_state

Controls randomness so results can be reproducible.


==================================================
17. FEATURE IMPORTANCE
==================================================

Random Forest can provide feature importance.

In scikit-learn:

model.feature_importances_


Example:

Area        → 0.50
Location    → 0.25
Bedrooms    → 0.15
Age         → 0.10

This tells us how much the model's splitting decisions
used each feature to reduce impurity.

Important:

Feature importance does NOT prove that a feature causes
the target to change.


==================================================
18. OUT-OF-BAG (OOB) SAMPLES
==================================================

Because each tree uses bootstrap sampling, some training
samples are not selected for a particular tree.

These are called:

OUT-OF-BAG (OOB) samples.

Example:

Original dataset:

A B C D E

Bootstrap sample for one tree:

A C C E E

B and D were not selected.

Therefore:

B and D
→ OOB samples for that tree.


These OOB samples can be used to estimate model performance
without using a separate validation set for that purpose.

In scikit-learn:

RandomForestRegressor(
    n_estimators=100,
    oob_score=True,
    random_state=42
)


==================================================
19. ADVANTAGES
==================================================

1. Usually more stable than a single Decision Tree.

2. Can model nonlinear relationships.

3. Can capture feature interactions.

4. Usually does not require feature scaling.

5. Works well as a strong general-purpose baseline.

6. Less prone to overfitting than a single fully grown
   Decision Tree in many practical settings.

7. Can estimate feature importance.

8. Can handle many features.


==================================================
20. LIMITATIONS
==================================================

1. More computationally expensive than a single
   Decision Tree.

2. Uses more memory because many trees are stored.

3. Less interpretable than one Decision Tree.

4. Predictions can be slower when the forest contains
   many trees.

5. Very large forests can become computationally expensive.

6. It does not naturally extrapolate well beyond the
   range of training targets.


==================================================
21. WHEN TO USE RANDOM FOREST REGRESSION
==================================================

Useful when:

- Target is continuous.
- Relationships are nonlinear.
- You have multiple features.
- You want a strong baseline model.
- You do not want to manually create polynomial features.
- Feature interactions may be important.
- You want a model less sensitive to overfitting than
  a single Decision Tree.


Examples:

Area + Bedrooms + Age + Location
              ↓
      Random Forest
              ↓
        House Price


Temperature + Humidity + Time
              ↓
      Random Forest
              ↓
      Energy Consumption


Experience + Education + Skills
              ↓
      Random Forest
              ↓
           Salary


==================================================
22. RANDOM FOREST vs POLYNOMIAL REGRESSION
==================================================

Polynomial Regression:

Features
   ↓
Create X², X³, ...
   ↓
Linear Regression
   ↓
Smooth mathematical curve


Random Forest:

Features
   ↓
Random samples
   ↓
Random feature subsets
   ↓
Many Decision Trees
   ↓
Average predictions
   ↓
Final prediction


Polynomial Regression:
→ Explicit polynomial relationship.

Random Forest:
→ Tree-based nonlinear relationships.


==================================================
23. RANDOM FOREST vs DECISION TREE
==================================================

Decision Tree:

Number of trees = 1
Prediction = one tree's prediction
Variance = generally higher
Interpretability = higher


Random Forest:

Number of trees = many
Prediction = average of tree predictions
Variance = generally lower
Interpretability = lower


==================================================
24. COMPLETE FLOW
==================================================

Dataset
   ↓
Features (X) + Target (y)
   ↓
Train / Test Split
   ↓
Random Forest Regressor
   ↓
Bootstrap Sampling
   ↓
